Indian foreign minister subramanyam  jaishankar is in Moscow and meeting  ministers including his counterpart  Sergey lavrov the two countries have a  complicated relationship Delhi has  indicated that it will try to push  Moscow for an end to the war in Ukraine  saying it's no good for the world but  since Russia's Invasion and Europe's  pivot away from Russian oil India has  been the one to pick up the slack and  Russia is now the country's largest  supplier of oil despite criticism India  has doubled down on its choice  it is our fundamental obligation to  ensure that the Indian consumer has the  best possible access on the most  advantageous terms to International  markets and in that respect uh quite  honestly we have seen that the India  Russia relationship has worked to  Advantage so if it works to my advantage  I would like to keep it in that keep  that going  India has also pointed out that Europe  continues to take more Russian crude oil  than India even now  Delhi choosing to maintain good  relations with Moscow underscores the  two countries long history together  India walks the line as a non-aligned  state notably it did not stand with the  group of democracies at the United  Nations when it came to condemning the  invasion of Ukraine here's DW's Chief  International editor Richard Walker to  explain more the UN Security Council  February 2022 the high table of world  affairs gathers to pass judgment on  Russia's invasion of Ukraine  her India's Ambassador is here holding  his country's non-permanent seat  he calls for a ceasefire we urge that  all efforts are made for the immediate  secession of violence and hostilities  the Western Powers vote to condemn the  war Russia casts its veto and India  abstains  what was India doing here it was joining  the likes of China in refusing to  condemn Russia's Invasion well this  wasn't a matter of principle this was  all about pragmatism  and not wanting to anger a valued friend  Delhi just two months earlier Vladimir  Putin on a rare trip abroad heaping  Praise on his hosts  the relationship has roots in the depths  of the Cold War  in 1971 Indra Gandhi and Leonid Brezhnev  were both concerned about tensions with  China  the result the indo-soviet Treaty of  Peace friendship and cooperation  India's policy of non-alignment meant  the treaty stopped short of being a full  Alliance but it helped to seal moscow's  position as India's number one arm  supplier something that's not been  forgotten  J panda is Vice President of India's  governing BJP party Russia or the former  Soviet Union had stood by India when we  needed defense capabilities to deal with  these challenges of China and others it  was the Soviet Union and continuing into  Russia that gave us equipment  fast forward to the end of 2021 and  Putin's visit to Delhi and arms were  very much on the agenda  Russia is supplying high-tech gear like  its s 400 air defense missiles to India  that deal is so sensitive that the  United States has threatened India with  sanctions for it but Delhi is sticking  to the deal Encounters of the West won't  match Russia on providing the military  Tech that it needs we are still denied  certain key defense Technologies which  are critical for our safety and for our  security and we have been getting them  from Russia Moscow has also done deals  allowing Indian manufacturers to make  Hardware like these Russian tanks that's  something other suppliers have been  reluctant to do  India not only gets the tanks it also  boosts its own defense industry a major  strategic priority  India's years-long Reliance on Russian  military hardware however isn't looking  so good almost nine months since the war  started in Ukraine Russian weapons have  not performed well on the battlefield  and that plus Russia's closer ties with  India's Regional rival China may explain  prime minister Narendra modi's recent  comments to Putin at an in-person Summit  to his face Modi said quote I know that  today's era is not an era of war and I  have spoken to you on the phone about  this and quote suggesting that the two  leaders had stronger words in private  to talk about all this we have harsh  Pond professor of international  relations with King's College London  thanks so much for joining the program  Professor can you parse what's happening  here because we see we've seen India  abstaining at the um but we've also seen  modi's recent tough comments to Putin  it's a quite a line to walk  yes indeed I think India has been  working a very very delicate balance  between its various Partnerships various  commitments and various kinds of  interests that are becoming Salient to  its foreign policy from defense to  energy to geopolitics and and I think  there is a growing discomfort in India  that the war that the conflict in  Ukraine has dragged on that there is no  site of a resolution yet and it is  having an impact you know globally in  economic terms and it is also certainly  having an impact on India's domestic  political economy itself so I think  India is now more vocal about the need  to bring it to an end talking directly  to Mr Putin as you mentioned but also on  the other hand trying to make the most  of the energy security as well as the  defense requirements for which it  continues to rely on Russia so it's  indeed a delicate balance  yeah definitely a geopolitical challenge  um I wonder if you can talk about how  China fits into all of this I mean India  and China have had border clashes in  recent years where soldiers on both  sides were killed but now we see Russia  and China cozing up where does that put  Delhi  in a very very difficult position and I  think uh that's why we are we are there  is a lot of concern in India about the  the the state of geopolitics at the  moment because uh you know as you were  mentioning India and China are at a very  very difficult position at this point  even as we speak soldiers are almost  eyeball to eyeball along the border  there has been some disengagement from  2020 uh when the when the escalation  happened when violence happened when  soldiers died but the de-escalation has  not really resulted uh in anything  substantive the the ties are very very  delicately poised at the moment and uh  the fact that Russia which is which has  been traditionally India's partner over  the last several decades especially  during the Cold War and now seems to be  becoming a close Ally of of China at a  time where India relies on Russia for  its defense supplies is very very I  think an extraordinary situation to be  end and therefore India perhaps is one  of the unique countries in the world at  the moment that needs Russia to manage  China at the at the border operationally  tactically as well as U.S and the West  to manage China more strategically at  the global level over the long term so I  think it's a very very difficult  position to be in diplomatically  yeah the foreign Ministry must be pretty  busy now India is also of course part of  the Quad that includes the U.S Australia  and Japan so how does it manage that  group of democracies with its  relationship uh with Russia and China is  it possible to do all of that  at least they are I think at the moment  trying to do that and and  uh I think the challenge for India  remains that uh it is facing pressures  from two sides uh the Continental  pressure from Eurasia where China is  pressing its claims along the border  along the Himalayan border and of course  you have the vast Maritime space that we  now call as the indo-pacific where again  we are witnessing a lot of geopolitical  contestation and India is trying to  partner with like-minded countries in  the wider indo-pacific so as to create a  stable balance of power in that vast  emerging geography and in that context  its relationship with quad members  becomes important because it's not  simply the U.S but also close allies of  the U.S like Japan and Australia that  are that make the court along with India  and India is partnering with these  countries to ensure that there is some  kind of stability in the maritime space  harsh Pond thank you so much for joining  us

